# Commit safety gate allowlist (specs/002-commit-safety-gate).
#
# One entry per line. An exact string suppresses that value wherever it
# matches. A line starting with "re:" is a regular expression: any value the
# pattern matches is suppressed. Blank lines and lines starting with "#" are
# comments, not entries. This suppresses matched VALUES, not whole files or
# lines: an unrelated occurrence of a different value on the same line, or
# the same value in a different file, is unaffected.
#
# For a one-off exception on a single line, use an inline "# scan:allow"
# marker on that line instead of adding an entry here.
#
# Seeded 2026-08-24 with the repository's own known-safe test fixtures
# (research.md D3). "Director Name" was removed 2026-08-25 (post-
# implementation review finding NIT-12): no pattern in this scanner can ever
# emit a plain two-word name, so it was never a real exception - just an
# unused line.
ABCDE1234F
director@example.com
super-secret-value-12345
hunter2-XY

# The three false positives discovered 2026-08-24 during T025's --history
# self-test (a Spec Kit template hash and a bash int64-max constant, both
# matched by the old, boundary-less phone_us pattern) and the
# generic_secret_assignment false positive on headless/fields.py:37 (a
# comma-and-quote-containing prose match, `, or 'literal:`) were removed
# 2026-08-25 (post-implementation review findings FIX-FIRST-5/6): each was a
# pattern-boundary bug, not a genuine exception, so it was fixed at the
# pattern (a digit-boundary assertion on phone_us; a "no embedded quote or
# comma" rule on generic_secret_assignment) instead of being allowlisted.
# See PATTERNS.md's "Commit safety gate" entry for the corrected guidance: a
# false positive on a non-secret shape is a pattern-boundary bug to fix, not
# a reason to allowlist; allowlist only genuine synthetic fixtures.

# 2026-08-25: two hex-hash substrings from .specify/integrations/speckit.manifest.json
# (a 10-digit run and a Luhn-valid 15-digit run, both flanked by letters) briefly
# needed entries here; fixed at the pattern instead by using alphanumeric boundaries
# ((?<![0-9A-Za-z]) / (?![0-9A-Za-z])) on phone_us, phone_in and payment_card, since a
# real phone or card number is never glued to a letter.
